DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Habermas' theory of modernity is the historical-diagnostic application of
his communicative framework. The thesis that modernity is an "unfinished
project" — first articulated in his 1980 Adorno Prize lecture — holds that
the differentiated value spheres of modernity (science, morality, art) have
not yet been adequately reconnected to communicative practice. Modernity
is not to be overcome (as postmodernists and neoconservatives argue) but
completed: the normative potential implicit in the differentiation of reason
must be realized through democratic deliberation rather than abandoned.

The theory draws on Weber's account of the rationalization of value spheres,
Parsons' systems theory, and Mead's intersubjectivism. The "colonization of
the lifeworld" is the pathological side of modernization; the task of a
critical theory of modernity is to articulate the normative conditions for
overcoming this colonization.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Domain 4 contains Habermas' substantive philosophy of history in its most
accessible form: the claim that modernity is incomplete rather than superseded
or exhausted. This is where his reconstructive alternative to speculative
philosophy of history meets concrete historical diagnosis, and it is the
locus of his ongoing dispute with postmodernism, neoconservatism, and
defeatist readings of the Frankfurt School tradition.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The key tension is between Habermas' normative confidence (the unfinished
project can be completed) and his diagnostic pessimism (colonization is real
and structural). Synthesis should map how Habermas positions the "unfinished
project" thesis against his postmodern and neoconservative critics, and
assess whether the normative residue of his theory of modernity can support
the weight he places on it.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Habermas' primary statements: 3 papers — PDM, Modernity lecture, TCA
- The "unfinished project" and its defenders: 3 papers — D'Entrèves/Benhabib,
  Honneth, Deen
- Social-theoretic infrastructure (Parsons, Luhmann): 2 papers — Chernilo,
  McCarthy
- Critics of the PDM reading of poststructuralism: 3 papers — Visker,
  Fleming, Schoolman
====================================================================
}

@book{habermas1987pdm,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen},
  title = {The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity: Twelve Lectures},
  year = {1987},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: In Lectures 1 and 2 Habermas traces the "discourse of
  modernity" from Hegel's first formulations through Nietzsche, Heidegger,
  Bataille, Derrida, and Foucault, arguing that each represents a failed
  attempt to exit modernity while remaining within its self-understanding as
  "a new age" that must find its normativity from itself. The book defends
  the thesis that modernity is an unfinished rather than exhausted project,
  and that the postmodern exit is premature.

  RELEVANCE: The fullest statement of Habermas' theory of modernity as an
  unfinished project, situated against postmodernist alternatives. Central
  to understanding both his historical diagnosis and his normative claims.

  POSITION: Core primary source (see also Domain 2 entry habermas1987philosophical).
  },
  keywords = {modernity, unfinished-project, postmodernism, Enlightenment, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{habermas2001modernity,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen},
  title = {Modernity: an Unfinished Project},
  booktitle = {Postmodern Debates},
  year = {2001},
  pages = {37--52},
  doi = {10.1057/978-1-137-04505-8_4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The text of Habermas' 1980 Adorno Prize lecture, which coined
  the phrase "modernity as an unfinished project." Habermas argues that the
  differentiation of reason into science, morality, and art — the cultural
  modernity diagnosed by Weber — has not been translated into the communicative
  practice of everyday life. The task is not to abandon the Enlightenment
  project but to reconnect these differentiated spheres to the lifeworld.
  The lecture explicitly positions this against neoconservative and
  postmodern attempts to declare modernity over.

  RELEVANCE: The founding document of Habermas' "unfinished project" thesis.
  Essential for understanding the normative content and historical ambitions
  of his theory of modernity.

  POSITION: Core primary source; the clearest and most accessible statement
  of the philosophy of history embedded in Habermas' theory of modernity.
  },
