  of history.

  POSITION: Critical secondary source; argues for resources in negative
  dialectics not captured by Habermas' communicative framework.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, aesthetic-reason, PDM, negative-dialectics, modernity, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{mccarthyideal,
  author = {McCarthy, Thomas},
  title = {The Critical Theory of Jürgen Habermas},
  year = {1978},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: McCarthy's critical introduction to Habermas' thought covers
  his early work on the public sphere, Knowledge and Human Interests, the
  theory of communicative competence, and the theory of social evolution.
  McCarthy argues that Habermas' project represents the most sustained and
  sophisticated attempt in twentieth-century social theory to reconstruct
  the critical potential of Enlightenment reason. He traces the development
  of Habermas' thought as a progressive working out of the implications of
  the communicative-action paradigm for social theory and philosophy of history.

  RELEVANCE: The standard secondary introduction to Habermas' theoretical
  development through the late 1970s. Essential for understanding the
  trajectory that leads from Knowledge and Human Interests to the Theory of
  Communicative Action and the theory of modernity.

  POSITION: Central secondary source; the most comprehensive English-language
  guide to Habermas' theoretical development up to the TCA.
  },
  keywords = {critical-theory, communicative-action, modernity, secondary-introduction, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

